WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.907
Memory safety is central to firmware
security because firmware often operates with

2
00:00:04.987 --> 00:00:08.339
constrained RAM, fixed flash layouts,
direct hardware access,

3
00:00:08.419 --> 00:00:10.157
and long-running control loops.

4
00:00:10.237 --> 00:00:14.395
A memory defect may appear only under an
unusual message, a rare reset sequence,

5
00:00:14.475 --> 00:00:18.758
a timing edge, or a field configuration
that was not present during early testing.

6
00:00:18.838 --> 00:00:20.825
Fixed buffers are common in firmware,

7
00:00:20.905 --> 00:00:23.574
but fixed size does not make a buffer safe
by itself.

8
00:00:23.654 --> 00:00:27.874
Code should validate lengths before
copying, formatting, appending, parsing,

9
00:00:27.954 --> 00:00:28.637
or indexing.

10
00:00:28.717 --> 00:00:32.441
Buffer sizes should be tied to protocol
definitions, hardware limits,

11
00:00:32.521 --> 00:00:34.880
file formats, or explicit design
constants,

12
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.815
not to old examples that nobody has
reviewed recently.

13
00:00:37.895 --> 00:00:40.068
Stack and heap policy should be explicit.

14
00:00:40.148 --> 00:00:43.562
Some firmware avoids dynamic allocation
after initialization.

15
00:00:43.642 --> 00:00:45.131
Some uses controlled pools.

16
00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:47.818
Some permits allocation in selected
subsystems.

17
00:00:47.898 --> 00:00:51.436
Whatever the policy is, it should address
failure, fragmentation,

18
00:00:51.516 --> 00:00:53.254
cleanup, ownership, and timing.

19
00:00:53.334 --> 00:00:57.741
Stack sizes should be supported by
measurement and by tests that exercise realistic

20
00:00:57.821 --> 00:00:58.442
call paths.

21
00:00:58.522 --> 00:01:01.501
Object lifetime and pointer ownership need
clear rules.

22
00:01:01.581 --> 00:01:04.250
A buffer passed from a driver to
application logic,

23
00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.441
from an interrupt path to deferred work,

24
00:01:06.521 --> 00:01:10.059
or from an update parser to storage code
should have a visible owner.

25
00:01:10.139 --> 00:01:13.118
The code should make it clear who may
read, who may modify,

26
00:01:13.198 --> 00:01:16.240
when the object is valid, and when it is
released or reused.

27
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.926
Integer sizing matters in hardware-facing
code.

28
00:01:19.006 --> 00:01:22.544
Device registers, bus lengths, packet
fields, counters, offsets,

29
00:01:22.624 --> 00:01:25.976
alignment rules, and flash addresses may
have specific widths.

30
00:01:26.056 --> 00:01:30.153
Conversions should be reviewed so that
signedness, truncation, wraparound,

31
00:01:30.233 --> 00:01:33.585
and mixed-width arithmetic do not change
the meaning of a check.

32
00:01:33.665 --> 00:01:38.072
Memory-mapped I/O and volatile access
should be isolated behind clear interfaces.

33
00:01:38.152 --> 00:01:41.814
Register access code should be careful
about ordering, side effects,

34
00:01:41.894 --> 00:01:46.115
reserved bits, read-clear behavior, and
concurrency with interrupt handlers.

35
00:01:46.195 --> 00:01:50.664
Higher-level firmware should call reviewed
driver functions instead of scattering

36
00:01:50.744 --> 00:01:53.289
direct register access across the
application.

37
00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:56.348
Parser discipline completes the memory
safety picture.

38
00:01:56.428 --> 00:01:59.345
Firmware parsers should reject malformed
input early,

39
00:01:59.425 --> 00:02:02.529
check lengths before reading fields, cap
loops and counts,

40
00:02:02.609 --> 00:02:04.595
handle unknown values deliberately,

41
00:02:04.675 --> 00:02:08.213
and keep partially parsed state from
influencing device behavior.

42
00:02:08.293 --> 00:02:10.900
Safe parsing is a design habit, not a
cleanup step.
